-- Socket Throughput Benchmark
--
-- This benchmark cannot be expressed as a plain cheatMain because tcpRead
-- requires the CLEAR fiber scheduler (epoll-backed yield on EAGAIN).
-- The equivalent Zig source is bench.zig — it exercises the identical code
-- path that the transpiler generates for tcpRead inside a fiber task.
--
-- runner.rb uses bench.zig when it is present alongside bench.cht.
--
-- WHAT WE ARE MEASURING:
--
--   C (baseline):  read() into char buf[4096] on the stack
--                  — 0 allocations, 0 pool ops
--
--   CLEAR (pool):  CheatLib.socketRead → ReadPool.acquire()
--                  — 0 GPA malloc calls, 1 bitmask ctz per read
--                  Slot released via restoreFrameMark (1 bitmask OR)
--
-- OLD CLEAR path (before ReadPool):
--   tcpRead → allocator.dupe(u8, buf[0..n])
--   = 1 GPA malloc per read × 100,000 reads
--   GPA involves: lock, free-list traversal, header writes, stats update
--
-- NEW CLEAR path (ReadPool):
--   tcpRead → pool.acquire() → @ctz(free_mask) — single bitmask instruction
--   100,000 reads = 0 GPA malloc calls in the hot path
--
-- Expected: CLEAR (pool) is on par with C or faster.
--   C uses fork() — separate processes with kernel IPC + wait() overhead.
--   CLEAR uses fibers in a single process — epoll event loop, no fork cost.
--   The allocation path (ReadPool vs GPA) is no longer the bottleneck.
--
-- @use_zig
